1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,261
You walked in for one thing.

2
00:00:02,261 --> 00:00:02,638
Milk.

3
00:00:02,638 --> 00:00:07,464
The simplest item in the building, the one almost everyone buys.

4
00:00:07,464 --> 00:00:13,950
And somehow, it's sitting as far from the front door as the store can possibly put it.

5
00:00:15,150 --> 00:00:22,418
To reach it, you pass the bread, the snacks, the flowers, the offers stacked at the end of every aisle.

6
00:00:22,418 --> 00:00:24,536
So the question writes itself.

7
00:00:24,536 --> 00:00:27,852
Who decided milk belongs at the back — and why?

8
00:00:30,150 --> 00:00:31,875
There's a famous answer.

9
00:00:31,875 --> 00:00:33,671
You've probably heard it.

10
00:00:33,671 --> 00:00:36,043
And it's right — but only partly.

11
00:00:36,043 --> 00:00:43,950
Because the real story starts a century ago, with a milk cart, a block of ice, and a store shaped like a maze.

12
00:00:45,150 --> 00:00:47,782
The famous answer goes like this.

13
00:00:47,782 --> 00:00:49,138
Milk is a staple.

14
00:00:49,138 --> 00:00:53,366
People buy it constantly, often several times a week.

15
00:00:53,366 --> 00:00:58,949
So if you put it at the back, every quick trip turns into a long walk.

16
00:01:00,150 --> 00:01:05,584
Retailers have a phrase for what happens on that walk: building the basket.

17
00:01:05,584 --> 00:01:07,323
You came for one carton.

18
00:01:07,323 --> 00:01:13,410
You leave with biscuits, a candle and a rotisserie chicken you never planned to buy.

19
00:01:15,150 --> 00:01:17,688
Store designers study this obsessively.

20
00:01:17,688 --> 00:01:23,806
Retail researcher Paco Underhill found shoppers barely register anything just inside the door.

21
00:01:23,806 --> 00:01:29,273
He called it the decompression zone — the stretch where your brain is still outside.

22
00:01:30,150 --> 00:01:32,526
By this logic, the milk is bait.

23
00:01:32,526 --> 00:01:37,872
The longest walk in the shop is a sales funnel with a fridge at the end.

24
00:01:37,872 --> 00:01:42,550
It's tidy, it's cynical — and it's missing something important.

25
00:01:45,150 --> 00:01:47,967
For most of history, there was no walk at all.

26
00:01:47,967 --> 00:01:56,665
In an early twentieth-century grocery, you stood at a counter, read out your list, and a clerk fetched everything from the shelves behind him.

27
00:02:00,150 --> 00:02:03,567
And milk often didn't come from a shop at all.

28
00:02:03,567 --> 00:02:05,127
It came to your door.

29
00:02:05,127 --> 00:02:13,671
Milkmen with horse-drawn carts, and later electric floats, made daily rounds through cities from London to Chicago.

30
00:02:15,150 --> 00:02:21,354
By the late sixties, Britain's milk floats made up the world's largest fleet of electric vehicles.

31
00:02:21,354 --> 00:02:27,053
In America, nearly thirty percent of consumers had milk delivered in nineteen sixty-three.

32
00:02:27,053 --> 00:02:29,775
By two thousand five: under half a percent.

33
00:02:30,150 --> 00:02:36,412
Delivery worked because milk spoiled fast and most homes had nowhere truly cold to keep it.

34
00:02:36,412 --> 00:02:42,331
Two things would end that world: a new kind of store, and a cold box in every kitchen.

35
00:02:45,150 --> 00:02:47,594
September sixth, nineteen sixteen.

36
00:02:47,594 --> 00:02:48,960
Memphis, Tennessee.

37
00:02:48,960 --> 00:02:55,430
A grocer named Clarence Saunders opens the first Piggly Wiggly and does something radical.

38
00:02:55,430 --> 00:02:59,241
He removes the clerk, and hands the shopper a basket.

39
00:03:00,150 --> 00:03:04,194
Saunders patented his self-serving store in nineteen seventeen.

40
00:03:04,194 --> 00:03:12,989
Shoppers followed one continuous path, shelves on both sides, and they passed every product in the store before they reached the cashier.

41
00:03:15,150 --> 00:03:19,223
That's the real ancestor of the milk-at-the-back theory.

42
00:03:19,223 --> 00:03:25,259
The first self-service store was designed, on purpose, to walk you past everything.

43
00:03:25,259 --> 00:03:28,533
The walk was the business model from day one.

44
00:03:30,150 --> 00:03:33,551
But in that maze, milk had no special spot.

45
00:03:33,551 --> 00:03:37,349
Most stores had no mechanical refrigeration yet.

46
00:03:37,349 --> 00:03:40,751
Dairy sat in iceboxes, or came off a wagon.

47
00:03:40,751 --> 00:03:42,808
The back wall wasn't cold.

48
00:03:42,808 --> 00:03:44,786
That was about to change.

49
00:03:45,150 --> 00:03:50,460
Through the nineteen-thirties, the electric refrigerator went from luxury to normal.

50
00:03:50,460 --> 00:03:58,109
In the United States, ownership jumped from about eight percent of homes to around forty-four percent in a single decade.

51
00:04:00,150 --> 00:04:04,140
A fridge at home meant you could buy a week of milk at once.

52
00:04:04,140 --> 00:04:11,524
The daily doorstep round began to fade, and the weekly shopping trip was born — by car, to a much bigger store.

53
00:04:15,150 --> 00:04:16,749
August nineteen thirty.

54
00:04:16,749 --> 00:04:25,164
In an empty garage in Queens, New York, Michael Cullen opens King Kullen: huge, cheap, mostly self-service, with parking.

55
00:04:25,164 --> 00:04:29,129
Many historians call it America's first true supermarket.

56
00:04:30,150 --> 00:04:39,973
Big stores needed refrigerated display cases, and those machines came with compressors, condensers and pipework — heavy, noisy and hot behind.

57
00:04:39,973 --> 00:04:42,532
Nobody wanted that by the front door.

58
00:04:42,532 --> 00:04:44,539
So the cold went to the back.

59
00:04:45,150 --> 00:04:46,957
Now follow the milk itself.

60
00:04:46,957 --> 00:04:58,541
It leaves the dairy in a refrigerated truck, held at around four degrees Celsius or colder, and arrives at the one door every supermarket has: the loading dock, at the back.

61
00:05:00,150 --> 00:05:01,211
Milk is heavy.

62
00:05:01,211 --> 00:05:10,007
A single US gallon weighs nearly four kilos, and stores move it by the pallet, often with several deliveries a week.

63
00:05:10,007 --> 00:05:13,950
Every metre it travels costs labour, and costs cold.

64
00:05:15,150 --> 00:05:18,459
So stores shrink that trip to almost nothing.

65
00:05:18,459 --> 00:05:21,916
Behind the dairy wall sits a walk-in cold room.

66
00:05:21,916 --> 00:05:27,727
Staff roll pallets straight in from the dock, and fill the shelves from behind.

67
00:05:30,150 --> 00:05:33,619
That's why the dairy section feels like a wall.

68
00:05:33,619 --> 00:05:35,095
It literally is one.

69
00:05:35,095 --> 00:05:43,657
The milk fridge is the front of a cold room with glass doors cut into it — and that cold room has to touch the dock.

70
00:05:45,150 --> 00:05:48,867
Look at convenience stores, from New York to Tokyo.

71
00:05:48,867 --> 00:05:55,500
There are no long aisles to march you past, yet the milk still lines the back or side wall.

72
00:05:55,500 --> 00:05:58,343
The cooler goes where the cold room is.

73
00:06:00,150 --> 00:06:02,805
So logistics put the milk at the back.

74
00:06:02,805 --> 00:06:07,976
But once it was there, store designers realised they'd been handed a gift.

75
00:06:07,976 --> 00:06:11,680
And they started designing the whole store around it.

76
00:06:15,150 --> 00:06:20,669
Many supermarkets follow a loop around the edge, sometimes called the racetrack.

77
00:06:20,669 --> 00:06:22,808
Fresh food lines the perimeter.

78
00:06:22,808 --> 00:06:25,914
Packaged goods fill the aisles in the middle.

79
00:06:25,914 --> 00:06:28,950
And the everyday staples sit at the far end.

80
00:06:30,150 --> 00:06:33,408
Fresh produce often greets you at the entrance.

81
00:06:33,408 --> 00:06:38,468
It's colourful, it smells good, and it makes the whole shop feel fresher.

82
00:06:38,468 --> 00:06:42,975
And the smell of the bakery drifts exactly where you'll catch it.

83
00:06:45,150 --> 00:06:49,438
Then come the end caps, the displays at the end of each aisle.

84
00:06:49,438 --> 00:06:54,418
Products placed there can sell far more than the same product mid-aisle.

85
00:06:54,418 --> 00:06:57,945
And the walk to the milk passes nearly all of them.

86
00:07:00,150 --> 00:07:01,898
Eye level is buy level.

87
00:07:01,898 --> 00:07:09,425
Brands pay for the best shelf spots, and children's cereal often sits lower, at a child's eye line.

88
00:07:09,425 --> 00:07:13,682
Every step toward the milk is another chance to be seen.

89
00:07:15,150 --> 00:07:19,527
Notice too that milk, eggs and bread are rarely neighbours.

90
00:07:19,527 --> 00:07:24,572
Spread them out, and one short list sends you round the whole track.

91
00:07:24,572 --> 00:07:26,798
The truck chose the back wall.

92
00:07:26,798 --> 00:07:28,950
The marketer chose the route.

93
00:07:30,150 --> 00:07:33,979
If this were only a trick, every store would play it.

94
00:07:33,979 --> 00:07:34,774
Not all do.

95
00:07:34,774 --> 00:07:43,950
Paco Underhill noticed supermarkets adding small grab-and-go sections near the entrance, for shoppers who just want in and out.

96
00:07:45,150 --> 00:07:53,921
Walk into a small city-centre store in London, Paris or Sydney, and you'll sometimes find milk in a chiller just inside the door.

97
00:07:53,921 --> 00:07:57,933
These stores serve commuters who want it in thirty seconds.

98
00:08:00,150 --> 00:08:09,079
But in the big weekly-shop stores, from Chicago to Manchester to Melbourne, dairy still sits along the back or side wall.

99
00:08:09,079 --> 00:08:12,252
Big stores, big deliveries, big cold rooms.

100
00:08:12,252 --> 00:08:13,950
Same answer every time.

101
00:08:15,150 --> 00:08:18,818
Where milk can move to the front, it sometimes does.

102
00:08:18,818 --> 00:08:21,427
Where it can't, it stays at the back.

103
00:08:21,427 --> 00:08:28,905
That pattern tells you which force came first: the geography of cold, not just the psychology of shoppers.

104
00:08:30,150 --> 00:08:33,218
Economist Russ Roberts made a sharper point.

105
00:08:33,218 --> 00:08:36,426
Moving the milk to the front isn't impossible.

106
00:08:36,426 --> 00:08:42,215
It means freestanding coolers, longer refrigeration lines and more restocking work.

107
00:08:42,215 --> 00:08:44,447
And someone has to pay for that.

108
00:08:45,150 --> 00:08:51,132
And shoppers, given the choice, tend to pick lower prices over a shorter walk.

109
00:08:51,132 --> 00:08:54,200
Stores that kept costs down stayed busy.

110
00:08:54,200 --> 00:08:57,805
In a way, we voted for the long walk ourselves.

111
00:09:00,150 --> 00:09:02,026
So: truck or marketer?

112
00:09:02,026 --> 00:09:03,732
Both, in that order.

113
00:09:03,732 --> 00:09:09,021
The truck put the milk at the back, because cold is expensive.

114
00:09:09,021 --> 00:09:13,969
The marketer kept it there, because your walk is valuable.

115
00:09:15,150 --> 00:09:24,968
Next time you walk in for one carton, you'll know what you're really doing: retracing a delivery route, a cooling pipe, and a hundred years of retail design, one aisle at a time.

116
00:09:30,150 --> 00:09:35,192
Because from up here, the path you follow every week was never really random.

117
00:09:35,192 --> 00:09:39,450
It was shaped, from the very start, by the thing you came in for.

118
00:09:45,150 --> 00:09:47,491
So here's a trick of your own.

119
00:09:47,491 --> 00:09:53,656
Make a list, grab a basket instead of a trolley, and walk straight to the back.

120
00:09:53,656 --> 00:09:55,841
The store built a racetrack.

121
00:09:55,841 --> 00:09:58,651
You don't have to run the whole lap.
